(Page 16 of 41 - Prototype to  Production) Tj T*
(Prototype to Production) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(16) Tj T*
(Here are four proven patterns that help teams build confidence in their deployments:) Tj T*
(* Canary: Start with 1% of users, monitoring for prompt injections and unexpected tool) Tj T*
(usage. Scale up gradually or roll back instantly.) Tj T*
(* Blue-Green: Run two identical production environments. Route traffic to "blue" while) Tj T*
(deploying to "green," then switch instantly. If issues emerge, switch back--zero downtime,) Tj T*
(instant recovery.) Tj T*
(* A/B Testing: Compare agent versions on real business metrics for data-driven decisions.) Tj T*
(This can happen either with internal or external users.) Tj T*
(* Feature Flags: Deploy code but control release dynamically, testing new capabilities with) Tj T*
(select users first.) Tj T*
(All these strategies share a foundation: rigorous versioning. Every component--code,) Tj T*
(prompts, model endpoints, tool schemas, memory structures, even evaluation datasets--) Tj T*
(must be versioned. When issues arise despite safeguards, this enables instant rollback to a) Tj T*
(known-good state. See this as your production "undo" button!) Tj T*
(You can deploy agents using Agent Runtime9 or Cloud Run10, then leverage Cloud Load) Tj T*
(Balancing11 for traffic management across versions or connect to other microservices. The) Tj T*
(Agent Starter Pack1 provides ready-to-use templates with GitOps workflows--where every) Tj T*
(deployment is a git commit, every rollback is a git revert, and your repository becomes the) Tj T*
(single source of truth for both current state and complete deployment history.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
