(Page 27 of 41 - Prototype to  Production) Tj T*
(Prototype to Production) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(27) Tj T*
(A2A - Reusability and Standardization) Tj T*
(You've built dozens of specialized agents across your organization. The customer service) Tj T*
(team has their support agent. Analytics built a forecasting system. Risk management) Tj T*
(created fraud detection. But here's the problem: these agents can't talk to each other -) Tj T*
(whether that be because they were created in different frameworks, projects or different) Tj T*
(clouds altogether.) Tj T*
(This isolation creates massive inefficiency. Every team rebuilds the same capabilities. Critical) Tj T*
(insights stay trapped in silos. What you need is interoperability--the ability for any agent) Tj T*
(to leverage any other agent's capabilities, regardless of who built it or what framework) Tj T*
(they used.) Tj T*
(To solve this, a principled approach to standardization is required, built on two distinct but) Tj T*
(complementary protocols. While the Model Context Protocol \(MCP22\), which we covered) Tj T*
(in detail on Agent Tools and Interoperability with MCP, provides a universal standard for) Tj T*
(tool integration, it is not sufficient for the complex, stateful collaboration required between) Tj T*
(intelligent agents. This is the problem the Agent2Agent \(A2A23\) protocol, now governed by) Tj T*
(the Linux Foundation, was designed to solve.) Tj T*
(The distinction is critical. When you need a simple, stateless function like fetching weather) Tj T*
(data or querying a database, you need a tool that speaks MCP. But when you need to) Tj T*
(delegate a complex goal, such as "analyze last quarter's customer churn and recommend) Tj T*
(three intervention strategies," you need an intelligent partner that can reason, plan, and act) Tj T*
(autonomously via A2A. In short, MCP lets you say, "Do this specific thing," while A2A lets you) Tj T*
(say, "Achieve this complex goal.") Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
59 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 60 0 R /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> >>
endobj
60 0 obj
<< /Length 1187 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
36 756 Td
13 TL
(Page 28 of 41 - Prototype to  Production) Tj T*
(Prototype to Production) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(28) Tj T*
(A2A Protocol: From Concept to Implementation) Tj T*
(The A2A protocol is designed to break down organizational silos and enable seamless) Tj T*
(collaboration between agents. Consider a scenario where a fraud detection agent spots) Tj T*
(suspicious activity. To understand the full context, it needs data from a separate transaction) Tj T*
(analysis agent. Without A2A, a human analyst must manually bridge this gap--a process) Tj T*
(that could take hours. With A2A, the agents collaborate automatically, resolving the issue) Tj T*
(in minutes.) Tj T*
(The first step of the collaboration is discovering the right agent to delegate to - this is made) Tj T*
(possible through Agent Cards,24 which are standardized JSON specifications that act as a) Tj T*
(business card for each agent. An Agent Card describes what an agent can do, its security) Tj T*
(requirements, its skills, and how to reach out to it \(url\), allowing any other agent in the) Tj T*
(ecosystem to dynamically discover its peers. See example Agent Card below:) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
61 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 612 792] /Contents 62 0 R /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> >>
