(Page 34 of 41 - Prototype to  Production) Tj T*
(Prototype to Production) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(34) Tj T*
(A2A and MCP are not competing standards; they are complementary protocols designed) Tj T*
(to operate at different levels of abstraction. The distinction depends on what an agent is) Tj T*
(interacting with. MCP is the domain of tools and resources--primitives with well-defined,) Tj T*
(structured inputs and outputs, like a calculator or a database API. A2A is the domain of other) Tj T*
(agents--autonomous systems that can reason, plan, use multiple tools, and maintain state to) Tj T*
(achieve complex goals.) Tj T*
(The most powerful agentic systems use both protocols in a layered architecture. An) Tj T*
(application might primarily use A2A to orchestrate high-level collaboration between multiple) Tj T*
(intelligent agents, while each of those agents internally uses MCP to interact with its own) Tj T*
(specific set of tools and resources.) Tj T*
(A practical analogy is an auto repair shop staffed by autonomous AI agents.) Tj T*
(1. User-to-Agent \(A2A\): A customer uses A2A to communicate with the "Shop Manager") Tj T*
(agent to describe a high-level problem: "My car is making a rattling noise.") Tj T*
(2. Agent-to-Agent \(A2A\): The Shop Manager engages in a multi-turn diagnostic) Tj T*
(conversation and then delegates the task to a specialized "Mechanic" agent, again) Tj T*
(using A2A.) Tj T*
(3. Agent-to-Tool \(MCP\): The Mechanic agent now needs to perform specific actions. It) Tj T*
(uses MCP to call its specialized tools: it runs scan_vehicle_for_error_codes\(\) on a) Tj T*
(diagnostic scanner, queries a repair manual database with get_repair_procedure\(\),) Tj T*
(and operates a platform lift with raise_platform\(\).) Tj T*
(4. Agent-to-Agent \(A2A\): After diagnosing the issue, the Mechanic agent determines a part) Tj T*
(is needed. It uses A2A to communicate with an external "Parts Supplier" agent to inquire) Tj T*
(about availability and place an order.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
