(Page 36 of 41 - Prototype to  Production) Tj T*
(Prototype to Production) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(36) Tj T*
(The primary benefit is human discovery--developers can search for existing tools before) Tj T*
(building duplicates, security teams can audit tool access, and product owners can) Tj T*
(understand their agents' capabilities.) Tj T*
(An Agent Registry applies the same concept to agents, using formats like A2A's) Tj T*
(AgentCards. It helps teams discover and reuse existing agents, reducing redundant work.) Tj T*
(This also lays the groundwork for automated agent-to-agent delegation, though this remains) Tj T*
(an emerging pattern.) Tj T*
(Registries offer discovery and governance at the cost of maintenance. You can) Tj T*
(consider starting without one and only build it when your ecosystem's scale demands) Tj T*
(centralized management!) Tj T*
(Putting It All Together: The) Tj T*
(AgentOps Lifecycle) Tj T*
(We can now assemble these pillars into a single, cohesive reference architecture! The life) Tj T*
(cycle begins in the developer's inner loop--a phase of rapid local testing and prototyping) Tj T*
(to shape the agent's core logic. Once a change is ready, it enters the formal pre-production) Tj T*
(engine, where automated evaluation gates validate its quality and safety against a) Tj T*
(Decision Framework for Registries) Tj T*
(Tool Registry: Build when tool discovery becomes a bottleneck or security requires) Tj T*
(centralized auditing.) Tj T*
(Agent Registry: Build when multiple teams need to discover and reuse specialized) Tj T*
(agents without tight coupling.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
